Mavergreen's own: every file that includes libc++'s <iostream> constructs a std::ios_base::Init, as
libstdc++'s does, so the standard streams exist before that file's global constructors run.

Why: clang22 links libc++ statically (clang++.cfg's portable-ld appends libc++.a after the program's
objects). Mach-O runs initializers in link order across objects; libc++'s init_priority(100) on its
stream initializer orders it only within its own object. So libc++'s streams were constructed after the
program's global constructors, and one that wrote to std::cout crashed (exit 139 on 10.9.5; libc++'s own
ios_Init.global.pass.cpp and iostream.objects/init.pass.cpp found it). Linked to libc++ as a dylib, dyld
initializes the library first, which is why Apple's toolchain, and upstream's tests on Apple, never meet
this. ios_base::Init::Init constructs the streams once, so the extra objects are cheap and harmless with
the libcxx22 dylib too.

Upstream's static-libc++ users on Apple would want it as well; to offer if upstream agrees on the shape.

tests/smoke-target.sh is the regression test (a program that writes to std::cout from a global
constructor, built with no flags and run where x86_64 runs).

--- a/libcxx/include/iostream
+++ b/libcxx/include/iostream
@@ -65,6 +65,15 @@
 extern _LIBCPP_EXPORTED_FROM_ABI wostream wclog;
 #  endif
 
+// Mach-O orders initializers across objects by link order (init_priority orders them only within one),
+// so with libc++ linked statically, after the program's objects, the streams were constructed after the
+// program's own global constructors ran, and using them there crashed. As libstdc++ does, every file
+// that includes <iostream> constructs an Init first; Init constructs the streams only once.
+// Mavergreen's own (patches/0006).
+#  if defined(__APPLE__) && !defined(_LIBCPP_BUILDING_LIBRARY)
+static ios_base::Init __mavergreen_ioinit;
+#  endif
+
 _LIBCPP_END_NAMESPACE_STD
 
 #endif // __cplusplus < 201103L && defined(_LIBCPP_USE_FROZEN_CXX03_HEADERS)
